{"id":"9c3989c0-e8e2-4492-ae4e-524639095124","object":"chat.completion","created":1784808485,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be a dict\\\")\\n    if \\\"text\\\" not in case or \\\"passes\\\" not in case or \\\"max_rounds\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"missing required fields\\\")\\n    text = case[\\\"text\\\"]\\n    passes = case[\\\"passes\\\"]\\n    max_rounds = case[\\\"max_rounds\\\"]\\n    if not isinstance(text, str):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"text must be a string\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(passes, list) or not passes:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"passes must be a non-empty list\\\")\\n    for p in passes:\\n        if not isinstance(p, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"each pass must be a dict\\\")\\n        if \\\"name\\\" not in p or \\\"old\\\" not in p or \\\"new\\\" not in p:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"pass missing name, old, or new\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(p[\\\"name\\\"], str) or not isinstance(p[\\\"old\\\"], str) or not isinstance(p[\\\"new\\\"], str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"name, old, new must be strings\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(max_rounds, int) or max_rounds < 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"max_rounds must be a non-negative integer\\\")\\n    current = text\\n    trace = []\\n    fixed_point = False\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            old = p[\\\"old\\\"]\\n            new = p[\\\"new\\\"]\\n            idx = current.find(old)\\n            if idx != -1:\\n                current = current[:idx] + new + current[idx + len(old):]\\n                trace.append(p[\\\"name\\\"])\\n                changed = True\\n        if not changed:\\n            fixed_point = True\\n            break\\n    return {\\\"artifact\\\": current, \\\"trace\\\": trace, \\\"fixed_point\\\": fixed_point}\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that implements a pass scheduler fixed point computation. The input case has a \"text\" (the artifact), a list of passes (each with name, old, new), and max_rounds. The task: apply passes in order repeatedly. Each pass performs at most one rewrite per sweep (the first eligible rewrite? Actually \"at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep\" meaning in each sweep, for each pass, find the first occurrence of \"old\" in the current text and replace it with \"new\"? But careful: \"Each pass performs at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep.\" So within one sweep (one full iteration over all passes), for each pass, we look for the first occurrence of old string in the current text, and if found, replace it with new string. Then move to next pass. After all passes, check if any change occurred. If no change, stop. Else repeat up to max_rounds. Also need to record passes that changed the artifact (application trace). Also return a fixed-point flag (whether we reached fixed point before max_rounds). The artifact after each change is updated for subsequent passes within the same sweep? The description: \"Apply passes in their registered order. Each pass performs at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep. Record only passes that change the artifact. Repeat full sweeps until none changes or the round budget is consumed.\" So each sweep runs all passes sequentially, each pass looks at the current text (which may have been modified by previous passes in same sweep). That's typical. Then after sweep, if no pass changed text, stop. Else continue next sweep. We have max_rounds, which is number of sweeps? Probably max_rounds is the maximum number of sweeps (full rounds). We'll assume max_rounds is the maximum number of full sweeps (or total passes? The description says \"round budget\" and \"Repeat full sweeps until none changes or the round budget is consumed.\" So max_rounds is number of sweeps. In fixture, max_rounds=8, passes=3, so up to 8 sweeps. Return value: JSON-serializable object. We need to output the artifact (final text), application trace (list of pass names that changed something, in order of occurrence? Actually \"Record only passes that change the artifact.\" Should be in order they were applied (i.e., the trace of changes). And fixed_point flag: bool indicating whether we stopped because no change (i.e., fixed point reached) or because max_rounds exhausted.\n\nAlso need to validate fields: text must be string, passes list must be non-empty, each pass has name, old, new (strings), max_rounds non-negative integer. Also must not mutate input case. Return dict with keys: \"artifact\", \"trace\", \"fixed_point\". Possibly also include nonce? Not required. The description says \"Return the artifact, application trace, and fixed-point flag.\" So we'll return dict with those three keys.\n\nEdge cases: overlapping replacements? For simplicity, find the first occurrence of old string after the current position? But the typical C-Reduce pass sweeps from left to right? The specification says \"first eligible rewrite per sweep\". That suggests we scan the text from beginning and replace the first occurrence of old. However, the old strings could be overlapping? But we are just doing simple string replacement: find first occurrence of old in current text, replace it with new. That's straightforward. After replacement, continue to next pass in same sweep. The next pass will look at the new text.\n\nWe must preserve order of passes as given. Each sweep applies passes in order.\n\nImplementation: define solve(case) -> dict. Validate. Then current_text = case[\"text\"]. passes = case[\"passes\"]. max_rounds = case[\"max_rounds\"]. Initialize trace = []. fixed_point = False. For round in range(max_rounds): changed_this_sweep = False. For p in passes: name = p[\"name\"]; old = p[\"old\"]; new = p[\"new\"]; if old in current_text: first_index = current_text.index(old); current_text = current_text[:first_index] + new + current_text[first_index+len(old):]; trace.append(name); changed_this_sweep = True. After loop over passes, if not changed_this_sweep: fixed_point = True; break. After loop, return {\"artifact\": current_text, \"trace\": trace, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point}.\n\nNote: If no change in a sweep, we break and set fixed_point True. If we exhaust max_rounds, fixed_point remains False.\n\nAlso must ensure that replacements are done in a single sweep sequentially. Also note: \"Each pass performs at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep.\" That implies we only replace the first occurrence, not all. We are doing that.\n\nValidation: raise ValueError if any required field missing or type wrong. But need to be strict: \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So check: case is dict, has \"text\", \"passes\", \"max_rounds\". text must be string. passes must be list of dicts each with \"name\", \"old\", \"new\" strings. max_rounds must be integer >=0.\n\nAlso \"preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\" – we include trace which has names.\n\nDo not mutate input: we work with copy of text.\n\nReturn JSON-serializable object – dict with strings, list, bool.\n\nNow produce the JSON response with single key \"implementation\". The Python source must be a string, properly escaped for JSON. We'll write the code with appropriate newlines and indentation.\n\nMake sure to use only standard library, no imports needed.\n\nWrite the solve function.\n\nLet me write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":994,"completion_tokens":1709,"total_tokens":2703,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":640},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1230},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":640,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":354},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"